   195	## 4. `(10)_1`: proof and normalization
   196	
   197	Use `A_1` for the reduced denominator of `L_1 delta_1`, where
   198	`L_1=lcm(den(delta_s),...,den(delta_2))`. This is exactly Moh's denominator
   199	increment specialized to the bottom.
   200	
   201	Why `L_1` appears: after the levels `s,...,2` have been fixed, the center
   202	`sigma_1` has exponents in `k((x^{-1/L_1}))`. Adding the term
   203	`pi x^{-delta_1}` requires adjoining `T=x^{-1/(L_1 A_1)}`. If
   204	`L_1 delta_1=a/A_1` in lowest terms, then `x^{-delta_1}=T^a`, and the Galois
   205	generator `T -> zeta T` multiplies `pi` by `zeta^a`, a primitive `A_1`-st root.
   206	The primitivity is because `gcd(a,A_1)=1`.
   207	
   208	The automorphism fixes `sigma_1`, preserves the bottom disc, and permutes the
   209	`a_1=eV_2` leading `pi`-coefficients of `p_g`. By TF-0 they are distinct. All
   210	nonzero orbits have size `A_1`; the fixed orbit has size 1 if present. Hence
   211	
   212	```text
   213	a_1=eV_2 == 0 or 1 mod A_1.
   214	```
   215	
   216	The "at most one root is 0" statement does not depend on an arbitrary centering
   217	of the picture. In the natural Moh chart `pi=(y-sigma_1)x^{delta_1}`, the fixed
   218	point of the multiplicative action is `pi=0`. If one translates the coordinate,
   219	the action becomes an affine rotation with one fixed point. Since the roots are
   220	simple, the fixed point can still occur at most once. The orbit count is
   221	coordinate-invariant; only the label "0" uses the `sigma_1`-centered chart.
   222	
   223	Moh's final bottom alternatives (12)/(13) imply this congruence. Type `(10)_1`
   224	as a corollary/necessary condition, not the verbatim p.201 print.
   225	
   226	## 5. TF-0
   227	
   228	Let `P=p_g`, `Q=p_f`, with `deg P=eV`, `deg Q=dV`, and
   229	
   230	```text
   231	d Q P' - e Q' P = gamma != 0.
   232	```
   233	
   234	If `r` is a double root of `P`, then `P(r)=P'(r)=0`, so the left side at `r`
   235	is 0, contradiction. The same argument handles a double root of `Q`. If `r` is
   236	a common root of `P` and `Q`, both terms vanish at `r`, contradiction. Thus
   237	both polynomials have simple roots and are coprime.
   238	
   239	This confirms the correction to D1-SUBTREE: `Q=p_f` also has simple roots.
   240	"Intra-f clustering below delta_1 is free" is false for the leading bottom
   241	coefficient; "invisible to pinned N" remains true.
   242	
