+Direct stance:
+
+1. The boundary program is done as a degree-ceiling program. It computes
+   `N` exactly and the computed value is `O(1)`: `JAC-FIBRE`,
+   `FRONTIER-EXACT`, `D1-PIN`, `D1-STAR`, `PIN-NOT-CEILING`, and
+   `ORTHO-DEFECT` are the right foundation, but they do not and cannot give
+   `D <= C(N)`. Integration #17 states the equality and the no-ceiling fact
+   explicitly (`xmodel/integration17-coordinator-fable51-20260902.md:14-40`);
+   the 1608Z synthesis already split "floor equals ceiling" from "degree
+   ceiling" (`xmodel/ideation-20260902T1608Z-synthesis.md:116-132`).
+2. The all-degree program should be this: start from the true
+   `(1)-(13)` superset plus integral pinned `N >= 6`, then test
+   realisability by a global interpolation/period engine. The core equation is
+   not local bottom-star existence. It is that the values
+   `F_i = c integral dx/g_y(x,tau_i) + a_i` on all roots of `g-c_2` interpolate
+   to a polynomial `f` of `y`-degree `< m`, with no forbidden powers in `x`;
+   the Sol review states exactly where the local version fails to be global
+   (`xmodel/time-function-endgame-review-sol56-20260902.raw.md:103-130`).
+3. Moh's missing "program" is very unlikely to be one more denominator or orbit
+   inequality. The live discriminator is an approximate-root normal-form
+   elimination: transform a surviving tower by Prop. 6.3/6.4 when the minor-disc
+   hypotheses allow it, get a smaller system with monomial Jacobian, then impose
+   polynomiality/cancellation in the approximate-root expansion. Appendix II does
+   exactly this on the page images: the first three p.202 cases are transformed
+   to smaller rows with Jacobian column `X`, `X`, `X^2`; the fourth branches to
+   a row with Jacobian `X^4` or an 11-variable transformed system. That is not a
+   passive census row. It is the first exact realisability calculation.
+
+Where I think the coordinator's framing is still slightly wrong: "find the
+missing restriction in Moh's program" sounds like a static arithmetic sieve on
+the 46-style approach map. I would reframe it as "compile Moh's Appendix II
+method uniformly." A static sieve may be a shadow and should be harvested, but
+the mathematical object is polynomiality in an approximate-root tower, equivalent
+to the global interpolation condition. That is the all-degree program.
