+## 1. Q1 -- Moh's Missing Elimination
+
+The decisive negative is settled enough to route work: `(1)-(13)` as printed
+leaves 658 rows in 63 `(n,m)` classes at `n <= 100`, where Moh's p.202 table has
+6 rows in 4 classes (`xmodel/census-rebase-opus5-20260902.md:342-391`). It
+must kill 652 excess rows, including the `(75,50)` excess `M_2 in
+{5,10,40,60}`, while retaining `(55,73)` with `V_2 in {2,3}`. Re-readings of
+`A_j` and forced `(10)` do not solve this: the stricter denominator readings
+kill four of Moh's six rows, and `NOT-ALL-(11)` cuts only 220 groups at
+`D <= 120`, empties nothing, and keeps too much (`xmodel/census-rebase-opus5-20260902.md:366-391`,
+`:926-930`).
+
+My proposed mechanism is:
+
+```text
+MOH-MONOMIAL-APPROX
+  For a (1)-(13) skeleton, use the major/minor disc split and approximate roots
+  T_i to transform the row into one or more smaller polynomial systems in
+  approximate-root coordinates.  The transformed Jacobian is a monomial x^r
+  rather than 1.  The row survives only if all negative-power terms cancel and
+  the declared degree windows for the remainders hold.
+```
+
+This is not a new theorem claim; it is the uniform form of what Appendix II is
+visibly doing. On p.207, after applying Prop. 6.4 to the first three cases
+where `u_3=d_3-v_3=1`, Moh transforms the table to smaller rows and records a
+Jacobian monomial. On pp.208-211 he chooses approximate-root normal forms
+`gbar=h^4+...`, `fbar=h^3+...` or, for the `(15,10,M_2=11,V_2=3)` row,
+`f=h^2+2 beta`, expands fractional powers of `f`, and derives polynomiality
+conditions such as degree bounds on the remainders. The final contradiction for
+that sample row is not an orbit count; it is that the equations force an
+incompatible `y`-degree.
+
+Cheapest test on the census:
+
+1. Implement an Appendix-II compiler for the six printed rows and the 652 excess
+   rows at `n <= 100`. It should output only the transformed signature first:
+   `(n',m',M'_*,V'_*, Jacobian monomial x^r, approximate-root degrees, remainder
+   degree caps)`.
+2. Require fail-closed calibration: all six printed rows must compile to the
+   signatures on Appendix II p.207/p.209, and the `(15,10,M_2=11,V_2=3)` sample
+   must reproduce Moh's 12-variable or 10-variable normal form and contradiction.
+3. Only after calibration, run the first polynomiality/cancellation rank test on
+   the 652 excess rows. If it kills exactly the excess at `n <= 100`, we have
+   recovered Moh's missing program. If it keeps an infinite family, those rows
+   become the counterexample-side target list. If it is genuinely case-by-case,
+   the campaign should stop looking for a Moh-arithmetic theorem and put the
+   proof side on the global interpolation/D-module form.
+
+Outcomes:
+
+* Exact kill of the 652 excess rows and the six Appendix systems: promote the
+  compiler as "Moh's missing program", then generalize the transformed
+  polynomiality equations to all degrees.
+* A strong but nonempty sieve: bank the sieve as target generation; it is not a
+  JC2 proof, but it gives a typed infinite or high-degree search frontier.
+* No stable sieve beyond `n <= 100`: Moh's program was a bounded endgame, and the
+  all-degree proof must use a datum outside the skeleton. The most plausible
+  datum is the global period/interpolation matrix, not a refined boundary count.
+
+## 2. Q2 -- The D = 105 Trio
+
+The three true `D=105` groups are all `(n,m,K,d,e)=(105,70,35,2,3)` with
+`V_2=1`; they differ only in `M_2,V_3,delta_1,delta_2,q,u`
+(`xmodel/census-rebase-opus5-20260902.md:481-487`). The bottom star is therefore
+the same rigid `(2,3,V=1)` Davenport-Stothers dessin in all three cases. After
+normalisation:
+
+```text
+p_g = pi^3 - pi,       p_f = pi^2 - 2/3,
+lambda_g = -3 mu,      lambda_f = -2 mu,      mu = (1-delta_1)/5.
+```
+
+The restricted one-disc linearized operator has the same count in all three
+groups:
+
+```text
+L_E : C[pi]_{<=2} plus C[pi]_{<=3} -> C[pi]_{<=4}
+generic E: rank 5, kernel 2, cokernel 0
+E in {0,mu,2mu,3mu}: rank 4, kernel 3, cokernel 1
+```
+
+This is the reviewed repair of the dead-row calculation, with the false
+"all multiples of mu" resonance removed
+(`xmodel/time-function-endgame-review-sol56-20260902.raw.md:474-533`). The
+local count alone will not kill the trio. The first possible cheap kill is
+global: at each exponent, the degree condition `deg_y f < m` gives
+`n-m = 35` high-coefficient equations. A nonresonant bottom disc contributes
+two local kernel parameters, before shared outer/minor variables and Galois
+identifications are imposed. Thus the first global rank test is already
+potentially overdetermined for two of the three groups and for the low-`N`
+part of the third.
+
+| group | data | `mu`, first outer gap | admissible bottom-disc count `k` | first-order count |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| A | `M=[28,103], V_3=5, q=1/2, u=25` | `mu=1/12`, `delta_1-delta_2=7/36` | `k=2N`, so `k=12,14,...,24` for `N=6..12` | `2k` unknowns vs `35` equations: count-kill possible for `N=6,7,8`; not by count alone for `N>=9` |
+| B | `M=[28,103], V_3=6, q=9/13, u=30` | `mu=3/26`, `delta_1-delta_2=7/26` | `k=13` for `N=9` | `26` unknowns vs `35` equations: first-order global rank can kill |
+| C | `M=[40,103], V_3=4, q=9/17, u=28` | `mu=3/34`, `delta_1-delta_2=11/34` | `k=17` for `N=9` | `34` unknowns vs `35` equations: razor first-order global rank can kill |
+
+Cheapest exact discriminator per group:
+
+* A: run the first global interpolation matrix for each `k=12,14,...,24`.
+  If the first order kills only `N<=8`, keep `N=9..12` as the best
+  counterexample-side target because it has enough local freedom to evade a
+  pure count. Since `u_s=2`, do not expect Prop. 6.4 to give the same immediate
+  minor transform as in Moh's first three Appendix rows.
+* B: use the global first-order rank test first. Because `u_s=1`, immediately
+  also compile the Prop. 6.3/6.4 monomial-Jacobian transform; its transformed
+  signature should be a sharp negative control against the `(75,50)` Appendix
+  row. A kill here is likely more informative than A because it tests the
+  Appendix-II mechanism.
+* C: launch first if a single trio row must be chosen. It has `u_s=1`, a
+  different `d_s=5`, and a first-order global count of `34` vs `35`. One
+  independent condition kills it; one rank dependency is a serious positive
+  signal. It also probes whether the `M_2=40` family, which Moh must kill at
+  `(75,50)`, is killed by the same mechanism above degree 100.
+
+A kill of all three groups buys only `D_min >= 108`. That is useful but not a
+flagship result by itself. A uniform theorem or compiler buys something larger:
+it converts the infinite `(1)-(13)` census into exact realisability tests, and
+it can decide whether the growing survivor list is proof-side dead or
+counterexample-side fuel. Therefore the flagship seat should not be "kill the
+trio by hand"; it should be "build the global interpolation/Appendix compiler,
+with the trio as the first target and Moh's six rows as controls."
+
+## 3. Q3 -- Reframing The All-Degree Program
+
+Disposition over the candidates:
+
+* Q3(a), D-module / Picard-Fuchs / differential Galois: raise and retype. The
+  right invariant is not "rank grows, therefore degree bounded" in the abstract.
+  It is the obstruction to representing the Gelfand-Leray form
+  `dx wedge dy / dg` by a polynomial primitive of `y`-degree `< m`. Equivalently,
+  the global Lagrange interpolant of `integral dx/g_y` must lie in `C[x,y]` with
+  `deg_y < m`. The cofinal quantity to test is the irregular part at infinity
+  of this connection restricted to the `(1)-(13)` tower. If the irregularity
+  supplies at least `n-m` independent high-coefficient conditions per order
+  after quotienting Galois orbits, this becomes a proof engine.
+* Q3(b), lattice plus bottom dessin tower: raise as a coupling receiver, not as
+  the main proof. `ORTHO-DEFECT` says `N` is a squared non-proportionality
+  defect; `STAR-ABC` says each bottom defect is a rigid Belyi/Davenport-Stothers
+  star. The new target is a "dessin tower" representation: a Galois orbit of
+  identical `(2,3,V_2)` or general `(d,e,V_2)` stars glued through the same
+  higher Puiseux tower. This can reduce the global interpolation matrix by
+  characters and expose missing conditions. I do not expect it alone to bound
+  `D`, because the proportional chain still stores unpriced length.
+* Q3(c), characteristic `p` / Cartier: retype as a diagnostic and a possible
+  accelerator. NO-RESIDUE is exactly the kind of statement Cartier can see:
+  the `x^-1` coefficient of `1/g_y` vanishes branchwise. But prior char-`p`
+  lifting has already warned that finite-field passes do not lift to polynomial
+  characteristic-zero maps. Use Cartier to produce rank certificates for the
+  interpolation matrix modulo good primes, not as an independent proof slogan.
+* Q3(d), semigroup at infinity / approximate roots: raise highest. This is the
+  best candidate for `OPEN[MOH-PROGRAM]` (bounded 652 excess rows at `n<=100`).
+  The page-image evidence points straight at approximate roots, quasi-
+  approximate roots, minor-disc transforms, and polynomial cancellation. The
+  all-degree version is a compiler for that structure.
+* Q3(e), counterexample-side construction: raise, but keep it honest. A high
+  order mod-`p` or Newton lift on group A at `N=9..12` would be the first real
+  positive signal above degree 100. A numerical formal solution is not a map;
+  it becomes valuable only if the same engine also kills Moh's Appendix rows.
+* Q3(f), theorem that the census never empties: raise/retype. The measured
+  `(1)-(13)` census grows through `D<=200` and `s<=5` already fails at `D=192`
+  (`xmodel/census-rebase-opus5-20260902.md:906-924`). I expect `(1)-(13)` plus
+  integrality to keep producing skeletons indefinitely. If so, proof must use a
+  datum not stored in the skeleton: global interpolation, approximate-root
+  polynomiality, periods, or source/target realisation.
+
+New avenue: monomial-Jacobian approximate-root compilation. Instead of trying
+to solve `[f,g]=1` in raw coefficients, transform the skeleton to Moh's
+Appendix-II coordinates and solve the smaller equations with a declared
+monomial Jacobian. This is simultaneously a proof-side filter, a faithful
+reconstruction of Moh's missing bounded program, and a counterexample-side
+normal form if it does not empty.
+
+New cross-connection: the same matrix should serve three roles that are
+currently separate: Q1's missing Moh filter, Q2's global interpolation kill, and
+Q3(c)'s Cartier/no-residue diagnostic. The operator is
+`d/dx f(x,tau)=1/g_y(x,tau)` before transform and
+`J_{x,y}(F,G)=x^r` after Moh's minor transform. Both are coefficient-level
+polynomiality conditions on a Puiseux/approximate-root expansion.
+
+## 4. Q4 -- Software Program
+
+Build `globalinterp` in the smallest useful version this week:
+
+```text
+Input:
+  one (1)-(13) skeleton, chosen branch multiset/k, target N, and a truncation order.
+
+Core:
+  1. Construct the bottom STAR-ABC normal form for each bottom disc.
+  2. Declare the exponent semigroup and separate inner unknown coefficients from
+     prescribed outer/minor contributions.
+  3. For every root tau_i of g-c_2, compute the series for 1/g_y(x,tau_i)
+     to the requested order, integrate formally, and attach a branch constant.
+  4. Build the Lagrange interpolant in y across all n roots symbolically or in
+     a Galois-character-compressed basis.
+  5. Emit the high-y coefficient equations y^70..y^104 and the no-negative-x
+     polynomiality equations order by order.
+  6. Optionally pass through Moh's Prop. 6.3/6.4 approximate-root transform and
+     replace the constant Jacobian by the monomial-Jacobian normal form.
+
+Output:
+  typed rank/cokernel counts per skeleton:
+  unknowns, equations, rank, forced compatibility equations, and whether the
+  pass is local, global-formal, or polynomial.
+```
+
+Gates:
+
+* Positive controls: automorphisms in `box/exactn-drivers-20260902` and the
+  simple `(y,x^j+y^k)` family must pass with the exact interpolant.
+* Negative controls: the seven non-Keller two-tower rows must fail the same
+  global criterion; Moh's `(15,10,M_2=11,V_2=3)` Appendix-II transformed row
+  must reproduce the degree contradiction visible on p.211.
+* Fail-closed ring checks: coefficient field, generator order, quotient/local
+  ring, exponent semigroup, and image maps must be printed in the report. No
+  name-matching `sat()` wrapper.
+* Preflight: any realisation job at `N>=6` consumes `box/preflight.py` first,
+  per `COORDINATION.md:721-723`.
+
+Smallest decisive experiment: group C at `D=105`, `N=9`, `k=17`, first global
+order. It is a one-condition-overdetermined rank test (`34` local bottom
+unknowns against `35` high-y equations before shared variables). If it kills,
+run B and A. If it passes by one exact rank dependency, immediately attempt a
+mod-`p` lift of group A at `N=12`, because that is the first nearby case where
+the count predicts enough freedom for a formal survivor.
